{"verdict":"allow","rev":14,"data":{"rev":14,"xml":"<doc node='280f32bb-af3e' rev='14'><paragraph id='mcj65xkpt52.0' rev='1' h='b664932f' heading='1'><text id='.1'>brain — system design</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.22' h='588da410'><date id='.23' value='2026-11-15'/><text id='.24'> · </text><mention id='.27' user='u_CWtHgBLpMH3GlqWmPX2Fqw' name='Chen Ohana' self='true'/></paragraph><paragraph id='.37' rev='2' h='d4b1ea57' heading='2'><text id='.38'>Overview</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.13608' rev='11' h='15b4cc70'><text id='.13609'><code>brain</code></text><text id='.13614'> is a personal CLI tool, built for the Endpoint "Shadow Assistant" home exercise, that captures everything typed on this Ubuntu machine and reconstructs it into searchable, queryable text.</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.13802' h='3caffc3e'><text id='.13803'>One binary, two commands:</text></paragraph><list id='.13828' h='82da4414' kind='bullet'><listItem id='.13829' h='a78e26db'><paragraph id='.13830' h='485e9ddf'><text id='.13831'><code>brain run</code></text><text id='.13840'> — captures keystrokes and focus changes globally, works out what was actually written in each app, and stores it.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.13954' h='091c7b0f'><paragraph id='.13955' h='a5117b4a'><text id='.13956'><code>brain query</code></text><text id='.13967'> — answers </text><text id='.13978'><code>search</code></text><text id='.13984'> and </text><text id='.13989'><code>text</code></text><text id='.13993'> questions about what's been captured, while </text><text id='.14038'><code>run</code></text><text id='.14041'> keeps going.</text></paragraph></listItem></list><paragraph id='.14054' h='8968fc3c'><text id='.14055'>The pipeline has three stages, matching the assignment's own split:</text></paragraph><list id='.14122' h='c8f6e92d' kind='ordered'><listItem id='.14123' h='011b58d3'><paragraph id='.14124' h='7aa6af26'><text id='.14125'><bold>Capture</bold></text><text id='.14132'> (hot path, must stay cheap): reads raw key events, resolves each one to a character using the live keyboard layout and modifier state, and appends it — along with focus-change events — to a durable, append-only log.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.14348' h='4785bcbe'><paragraph id='.14349' h='71239b47'><text id='.14350'><bold>Ingest</bold></text><text id='.14356'> (cold path): tails that log, groups events into writing sessions, and reconstructs each session's text once it closes, writing the result into a queryable, full-text-indexed store.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.14537' h='3f0c9d20'><paragraph id='.14538' h='18f3338e'><text id='.14539'><bold>Query</bold></text><text id='.14544'>: reads that store — </text><text id='.14565'><code>search</code></text><text id='.14571'> for full-text lookups across sessions, </text><text id='.14611'><code>text</code></text><text id='.14615'> for the raw captured stream over a time window — concurrently with </text><text id='.14683'><code>run</code></text><text id='.14686'>, always seeing a consistent state.</text></paragraph></listItem></list><paragraph id='.14721' h='a84287bf'><text id='.14722'>Reconstruction uses a tiered strategy depending on the app:</text></paragraph><list id='.14781' h='c7a974f5' kind='bullet'><listItem id='.14782' h='34bdaaf5'><paragraph id='.14783' h='e223188e'><text id='.14784'><bold>Tier 0 — generic buffer replay</bold></text><text id='.14814'> (always available): the decoded character stream is replayed through a virtual cursor/selection text buffer (backspace, delete, arrows, Ctrl+A, paste-from-clipboard-log). Covers any app adequately, including the terminal and Chrome in this build.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.15061' h='558ad7a0'><paragraph id='.15062' h='88639909'><text id='.15063'><bold>Tier 1 — static per-app rules</bold></text><text id='.15092'>: documented formatting conventions layered on top of tier 0, where it's cheap to add.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.15178' h='871e0a34'><paragraph id='.15179' h='03c25d25'><text id='.15180'><bold>Tier 2 — real-app replay</bold></text><text id='.15204'>: for VS Code only in this build, replay the actual captured keystrokes into a sandboxed, invisible instance of the real editor and read back its own output.</text></paragraph></listItem></list><paragraph id='.15361' h='e46181c8'><text id='.15362'>Every session gets a usable reconstruction regardless of tier — tier 2 is additive precision for VS Code, never a dependency for anything else to work.</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.23875' rev='12' h='1218dc50' heading='2'><text id='.23876'>CLI reference</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.23889' h='96df66ae'><text id='.23890'>Both commands take </text><text id='.23909'><code>--data-dir &lt;dir&gt;</code></text><text id='.23925'> (default </text><text id='.23935'><code>./brain-data</code></text><text id='.23947'>) and exit non-zero with a message on stderr on error.</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.24001' h='2510b72f' heading='3'><text id='.24002'><code>brain run</code></text></paragraph><codeBlock id='.24011' h='2191d862' language='bash'><text id='.24012'><![CDATA[brain run [--data-dir ]]>&lt;<![CDATA[dir>]]]></text></codeBlock><paragraph id='.24040' h='99db8933'><text id='.24041'>Runs until stopped (Ctrl+C or killed). Captures continuously; no other flags.</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.24118' h='634e42a7' heading='3'><text id='.24119'><code>brain query search</code></text></paragraph><codeBlock id='.24137' h='b080e38a' language='bash'><text id='.24138'><![CDATA[brain query search "]]>&lt;<![CDATA[query>" [--app A] [--from T] [--to T] [--limit N] [--data-dir ]]>&lt;<![CDATA[dir>]]]></text></codeBlock><paragraph id='.24227' h='8cd702bb'><text id='.24228'>One or more words, case-insensitive, ANDed together — every word must appear in the same session's reconstructed text. Prints JSON:</text></paragraph><codeBlock id='.24359' h='5ba2b822' language='json'><text id='.24360'><![CDATA[{
  "results": [
    {
      "session_id": "3f9a1c20",
      "app": "com.tinyspeck.slackmacgap",
      "started_at": "2026-11-08T14:03:21.950Z",
      "ended_at": "2026-11-08T14:09:51.004Z",
      "snippet": "…the migration is blocked on the schema change…"
    }
  ]
}]]></text></codeBlock><paragraph id='.24629' h='2d120f78'><text id='.24630'>A word typed and fully deleted within the same session never appears here; a word present when a session closed stays findable through that session's record even if a later session on the same app deletes it from the live document — see Session definition below.</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.24892' h='f99a4704' heading='3'><text id='.24893'><code>brain query text</code></text></paragraph><codeBlock id='.24909' h='a0b67e7d' language='bash'><text id='.24910'><![CDATA[brain query text --app A [--at T] [--from T] [--to T] [--data-dir ]]>&lt;<![CDATA[dir>]]]></text></codeBlock><paragraph id='.24982' h='14e066fa'><text id='.24983'>Returns the </text><text id='.24995'><bold>raw decoded keystroke stream</bold></text><text id='.25023'> for app </text><text id='.25032'><code>A</code></text><text id='.25033'> — exactly the characters captured in order, with no backspace/cursor editing applied — over a time window:</text></paragraph><list id='.25140' h='99ac1395' kind='bullet'><listItem id='.25141' h='4248b524'><paragraph id='.25142' h='f7593eef'><text id='.25143'>No time flag given: the last 10 minutes, ending now.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.25195' h='46e2e7a8'><paragraph id='.25196' h='d161a938'><text id='.25197'><code>--at T</code></text><text id='.25203'>: a 10-minute window ending at </text><text id='.25234'><code>T</code></text><text id='.25235'> (equivalent to </text><text id='.25251'><code>--to T</code></text><text id='.25257'> with the default window length).</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.25290' h='c833346b'><paragraph id='.25291' h='e7ddc812'><text id='.25292'><code>--from T</code></text><text id='.25300'> / </text><text id='.25303'><code>--to T</code></text><text id='.25309'>: an explicit window; either bound can be given alone.</text></paragraph></listItem></list><codeBlock id='.25363' h='66b50abd' language='json'><text id='.25364'><![CDATA[{ "app": "com.apple.Notes", "at": null, "text": "Goals\n- ship search\n- …" }]]></text></codeBlock><paragraph id='.25441' h='ba600e80'><text id='.25442'>Deliberately distinct from </text><text id='.25469'><code>search</code></text><text id='.25475'>'s session-reconstructed, edit-aware text — see Decisions log below for why the two diverge.</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.25567' h='73302524' heading='2'><text id='.25568'>Session definition</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.25586' h='06a6ae6e'><text id='.25587'>A session is keyed by </text><text id='.25609'><code>(app, window/context identity)</code></text><text id='.25639'>. One session accumulates all keystrokes typed into that specific context until it closes.</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.25729' h='9c741c44'><text id='.25730'><bold>A session closes when:</bold></text></paragraph><list id='.25752' h='79cd32d1' kind='bullet'><listItem id='.25753' h='ef6b824f'><paragraph id='.25754' h='783d3d5c'><text id='.25755'>30 seconds pass with no keystrokes in that context (idle timeout), or</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.25824' h='e0fbdf71'><paragraph id='.25825' h='ca8cb204'><text id='.25826'>a commit key fires in a single-line context — Enter in a terminal shell prompt, a chat compose box, or a browser address bar — closing it immediately, even before the idle timeout.</text></paragraph></listItem></list><paragraph id='.26006' h='ae0b0d72'><text id='.26007'><bold>A session does not close on focus loss.</bold></text><text id='.26046'> Switching to another app or window pauses that context's session without ending it. If the user returns to the same </text><text id='.26163'><code>(app, window)</code></text><text id='.26176'> context before its 30-second idle timeout has elapsed — counted from that context's own last keystroke, regardless of what happened elsewhere in between — the same session resumes and keeps accumulating. Only once 30 seconds of inactivity in that specific context has passed does it close; a later return to that context starts a new one.</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.26515' h='5b233a5d'><text id='.26516'><bold>Single-line vs. multi-line contexts</bold></text><text id='.26551'> are distinguished by a small, explicit app-class table (terminal prompts, chat compose boxes, and address/search bars are single-line; code editors, notes apps, and multi-line text areas are not) — Enter only acts as a commit key in the former; in the latter it's literal text passed through tier 0 like any other character.</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.26876' h='1ca5d701'><text id='.26877'><bold>Why this shape</bold></text><text id='.26891'>: it matches how people actually write — a paragraph composed with a few seconds' pause to think, or a quick alt-tab to check something and back, reads as one session; walking away for a coffee, or genuinely switching tasks, correctly starts a new one. It also keeps session boundaries decoupled from window-manager focus churn, which is noisy and not a reliable signal of writing intent by itself.</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.1755' rev='3' h='59f183a7' heading='2'><text id='.1756'>Data model</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.15513' rev='11' h='db78fdb1'><text id='.15514'>The capture log is the ground truth; SQLite is a derived, rebuildable query index fed by ingest.</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.15610' h='6050f403'><text id='.15611'><bold>Capture log</bold></text><text id='.15622'> (append-only JSONL, one line per event, matching the assignment's own example format):</text></paragraph><codeBlock id='.15709' h='1be07745' language='json'><text id='.15710'><![CDATA[{"ts":"2026-11-08T14:03:22.118Z","app":"org.example.app","type":"key","key":"H","mods":["shift"]}
{"ts":"2026-11-08T14:03:25.400Z","app":"org.example.app","type":"focus","window":"Untitled — Notes"}]]></text></codeBlock><paragraph id='.15908' h='a9f3744e'><text id='.15909'>Each line is complete and independently parseable, appended with a trailing newline and fsync'd in small batches. A </text><text id='.16025'><code>kill -9</code></text><text id='.16032'> mid-write can leave at most one torn trailing line, which ingest detects (invalid JSON or missing newline) and discards on restart — never a corrupted or ambiguous history.</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.16205' h='0725d512'><text id='.16206'><bold>SQLite (WAL mode, derived)</bold></text><text id='.16232'>:</text></paragraph><codeBlock id='.16233' h='ac7bec61' language='sql'><text id='.16234'><![CDATA[PRAGMA journal_mode = WAL;

CREATE TABLE sessions (
  id                 INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,
  app                TEXT NOT NULL,
  window             TEXT NOT NULL,
  started_at         TEXT NOT NULL,   -- RFC3339, matches the log's ts format
  ended_at           TEXT,
  status             TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'open',   -- open | closed
  tier_used          INTEGER,
  reconstructed_text TEXT
);
CREATE INDEX idx_sessions_app_time ON sessions(app, started_at);
CREATE INDEX idx_sessions_status ON sessions(status);

CREATE VIRTUAL TABLE sessions_fts USING fts5(
  reconstructed_text, content='sessions', content_rowid='id',
  tokenize='porter unicode61'
);
-- + AFTER INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE triggers keep sessions_fts in sync.

CREATE TABLE ingest_state (
  id         INTEGER PRIMARY KEY CHECK (id = 1),
  log_offset INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0   -- byte offset into the capture log already ingested
);

CREATE TABLE log_checkpoints (
  app         TEXT NOT NULL,
  ts          TEXT NOT NULL,
  byte_offset INTEGER NOT NULL,
  PRIMARY KEY (app, ts)
);]]></text></codeBlock><paragraph id='.17287' h='8d6cd988'><text id='.17288'>Raw keystrokes aren't duplicated into a SQLite table at all — </text><text id='.17350'><code>query text</code></text><text id='.17360'> reads the capture log directly for a given app and time window, using </text><text id='.17431'><code>log_checkpoints</code></text><text id='.17446'> (written every few seconds per app) to seek near the right byte offset instead of scanning from the start.</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.3724' h='4e7c6edb' heading='2'><text id='.3725'>Capture, </text><text id='.23800'>inges</text><text id='.3742'>tion and concurrency</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.17553' h='38f5fd9b'><text id='.17554'><bold>Hot path</bold></text><text id='.17562'> (runs per keystroke, must stay cheap): read the raw key event, resolve it to a character using the live XKB keymap and current modifier state (</text><text id='.17706'><code>key</code></text><text id='.17709'> already reflects shift/caps-lock/layout, per the capture-time resolution decided for this build), and append one JSON line to the capture log — plus one line per focus-change event. Small batched fsyncs (every ~50ms or N events) bound both latency and syscall overhead.</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.17979' h='65b86657'><text id='.17980'><bold>Ingest</bold></text><text id='.17986'> (cold path, same </text><text id='.18004'><code>brain run</code></text><text id='.18013'> process): tails the capture log from the last </text><text id='.18060'><code>ingest_state.log_offset</code></text><text id='.18083'>, assigns each event to a session per the session-definition rules, and advances the offset in the same SQLite transaction as any session data it writes — offset and data can never drift apart, even across a crash. On startup, ingest always resumes from the persisted offset, re-processing only what wasn't yet committed.</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.18404' h='2b6881a7'><text id='.18405'><bold>Concurrency</bold></text><text id='.18416'>: SQLite in WAL mode — the one writer (ingest) never blocks readers, and readers (</text><text id='.18498'><code>brain query</code></text><text id='.18509'>) never block the writer; each reader sees a consistent snapshot as of when its read began, never a half-written session. </text><text id='.18631'><code>brain query text</code></text><text id='.18647'> reads the capture log directly for the given app and time window; </text><text id='.18714'><code>brain query search</code></text><text id='.18732'> hits </text><text id='.18738'><code>sessions_fts</code></text><text id='.18750'> for everything already closed and reconstructed, plus a just-in-time tier-0 reconstruction of whatever session is currently open for a live-search path.</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.18903' h='0924b2be'><text id='.18904'><bold>Reconstruction</bold></text><text id='.18918'>: once a session closes, ingest reconstructs it (tier 0/1 inline; tier 2 for VS Code handed to a small, separately bounded task with a timeout, falling back to tier 0 on failure) and writes </text><text id='.19108'><code>reconstructed_text</code></text><text id='.19126'>, which the FTS5 trigger indexes automatically.</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.5444' rev='5' h='bb241343' heading='2'><text id='.5445'>Reconstruction pipeline</text></paragraph><embed id='.19173' rev='11' h='31351832' caption='reconstruction dispatch · VS Code is the only tier-2 app' ref='node/379f9a1a-d68a'/><paragraph id='.19174' h='0e278685'><text id='.19175'>A tier 2 failure or timeout falls back to tier 0 for that session rather than losing it — tier 2 is additive precision for VS Code, never a hard dependency. Every adapter implements the same interface so the pool can treat them uniformly:</text></paragraph><codeBlock id='.19413' h='7270d54c' language='rust'><text id='.19414'><![CDATA[trait Tier2Adapter {
    fn reconstruct(&self, session: &Session, keystrokes: &[Keystroke]) -> Result]]>&lt;<![CDATA[String, ReconstructError>;
}]]></text></codeBlock><paragraph id='.5869' h='2d75bc9a' heading='2'><text id='.23805'>Chrome and the terminal: tier 0/1 only</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.19544' h='cd5f4ef3'><text id='.19545'>Earlier drafts of this design sandboxed a real, invisible instance of each app (Chrome via the DevTools Protocol, the terminal via a headless pty replaying into the user's real shell) to get an exact replay. For this build, both are scoped back to tier 0/1 — the same generic buffer-replay engine as every other app — to keep implementation effort proportionate to a take-home rather than a production system; VS Code is the one case where the extra precision was judged worth it (below).</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.20033' h='323b3bb0'><text id='.20034'><bold>What this costs</bold></text><text id='.20049'>: Chrome and the terminal lose exact handling of app-specific behavior — a web page's own autocomplete/autoformat JS, or a shell's readline history search and tab completion — that only the real engine reproduces faithfully. Tier 0 still correctly handles plain typing, backspace, cursor movement and Ctrl+V-from-clipboard-log for both.</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.20385' h='fffae757'><text id='.20386'><bold>Future work</bold></text><text id='.20397'> (see Known limitations): the full sandboxed-replay design for both is kept here as the path to exact reconstruction if this is extended beyond the exercise.</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.20554' h='fe9f121d'><text id='.20555'><italic>Chrome, sketched</italic></text><text id='.20571'>: sandbox on an isolated, network-isolated profile with </text><text id='.20627'><code>--remote-debugging-port</code></text><text id='.20650'>; dispatch keystrokes via CDP's </text><text id='.20682'><code>Input.dispatchKeyEvent</code></text><text id='.20704'> into the focused element (needs a companion extension logging </text><text id='.20767'><code>url</code></text><text id='.20770'> plus a stable selector per focus change, since a page title alone doesn't disambiguate a field); read back via </text><text id='.20882'><code>Runtime.evaluate</code></text><text id='.20898'>. The browser's own native UI (the omnibox) isn't page DOM, so it would need AT-SPI instead.</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.20990' h='8d5ba44b'><text id='.20991'><italic>Terminal, sketched</italic></text><text id='.21009'>: no </text><text id='.21014'><code>Xvfb</code></text><text id='.21018'> needed — open a pty, spawn the user's real shell with their real </text><text id='.21084'><code>.bashrc</code></text><text id='.21091'>/</text><text id='.21092'><code>inputrc</code></text><text id='.21099'> loaded, feed it the session's keystrokes, and parse the ANSI output with a headless terminal-state emulator (e.g. </text><text id='.21214'><code>alacritty_terminal</code></text><text id='.21232'>'s grid, unrendered) to read the current line. Only applies to bare shell-prompt sessions; a full-screen program (vim, </text><text id='.21351'><code>less</code></text><text id='.21355'>) taking over the terminal needs its own handling.</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.7710' rev='7' h='ce2e5cb7' heading='2'><text id='.7711'>Tier 2 adapter: VS Code</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.7734' h='77eacccc'><text id='.7735'>VS Code is Electron, so the same CDP mechanism as Chrome applies, with one important difference: there's no need to separately parse </text><text id='.7868'><code>keybindings.json</code></text><text id='.7884'> and reimplement VS Code's keybinding resolution (including the </text><text id='.7948'><code>when</code></text><text id='.7952'>-clause context problem noted earlier) — running the real app with the user's real config resolves all of that correctly, for free.</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.8083' h='55e22ba8'><text id='.8084'>Launch headlessly on </text><text id='.8105'><code>Xvfb</code></text><text id='.8109'>, pointed at the user's actual config:</text></paragraph><codeBlock id='.8147' h='e20f9a93' language='bash'><text id='.8148'><![CDATA[DISPLAY=:99 code \
  --user-data-dir=/var/lib/brain/replay/vscode-userdata \
  --extensions-dir=]]>&lt;<![CDATA[copy of the user's real extensions dir> \
  ]]>&lt;<![CDATA[file path parsed from the session's window_title>]]></text></codeBlock><paragraph id='.8339' h='1f528c26'><text id='.8340'>VS Code's title bar already includes the open file's path in </text><text id='.8401'><code>window_title</code></text><text id='.8413'>, so — unlike Chrome — no companion capture extension is needed to know what to open. Replay the keystrokes via CDP </text><text id='.8529'><code>Input</code></text><text id='.8534'> events into the focused Monaco editor, exactly as the real app received them, so every keybinding (including multi-key chords and extension-contributed commands) resolves the same way it did originally.</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.8737' h='19c8b8e4'><text id='.8738'><bold>Reading the result back</bold></text><text id='.8761'>: rather than reaching into Monaco's internal model through </text><text id='.8821'><code>Runtime.evaluate</code></text><text id='.8837'> (fragile — it's not a stable public API), let the replay finish with a real </text><text id='.8914'><code>Ctrl+S</code></text><text id='.8920'> against an isolated scratch copy of the file, then just read that file off disk. Simpler and more robust than DOM/JS introspection.</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.9052' h='8817cdae'><text id='.9053'><bold>Known gap</bold></text><text id='.9062'>: the file's content at the </text><text id='.9090'><italic>start</italic></text><text id='.9095'> of the session must match what the user actually had open — </text><comment id='9bd9f3cb-82f4'/><text id='.9156'>snapshot the file (or the relevant git blob) when the session opens, rather than assuming the current on-disk content still matches, since other edits can happen between then and reconstruction time.</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.21405' rev='11' h='e49b5fce'><text id='.21406'>This is the only tier 2 adapter built for this exercise — see "Chrome and the terminal" above for why those two stay at tier 0/1, and Known limitations for the trade-off this implies.</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.11129' h='2c333059' heading='2'><text id='.11130'>Safety</text><text id='.23843'>, resource limits and sleep/wake</text></paragraph><list id='.21589' h='de4cbfa3' kind='bullet'><listItem id='.21590' h='725bba52'><paragraph id='.21591' h='afeffee0'><text id='.21592'><bold>Resource budget</bold></text><text id='.21607'>: the assignment requires ≤1% average CPU while typing and ≤32MB memory for </text><text id='.21683'><code>run</code></text><text id='.21686'>. The hot path (evdev read, one XKB lookup, one JSON-line append) should hold this comfortably — verify it directly rather than assuming (see Testing and verification below).</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.21860' h='84ab35e3'><paragraph id='.21861' h='0ab1e28d'><text id='.21862'><bold>Sleep/wake</bold></text><text id='.21872'>: evdev file descriptors and the focus-tracking connection can go stale across a suspend/resume cycle. Subscribe to </text><text id='.21988'><code>systemd-logind</code></text><text id='.22002'>'s </text><text id='.22005'><code>PrepareForSleep</code></text><text id='.22020'> D-Bus signal; on the resume edge, re-open the input device handles and the focus-tracking connection before resuming capture.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.22146' h='45834310'><paragraph id='.22147' h='caeb52fb'><text id='.22148'><bold>Network isolation</bold></text><text id='.22165'> is mandatory for the VS Code tier 2 adapter if its extensions have live network access — a sandboxed-but-connected replay that triggers a sync or telemetry call is a real side effect, not just a reconstruction.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.22376' h='c83e6f8f'><paragraph id='.22377' h='e0413626'><text id='.22378'><bold>One warm instance</bold></text><text id='.22395'>, not one per session — Electron startup cost is real; keep the VS Code sandbox running and reset it (fresh scratch file) between sessions.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.22534' h='b74d7ac6'><paragraph id='.22535' h='8c3f73db'><text id='.22536'><bold>Resource caps</bold></text><text id='.22549'>: the VS Code sandbox runs under a </text><text id='.22584'><code>systemd-run --scope</code></text><text id='.22603'> cgroup with </text><text id='.22616'><code>CPUQuota</code></text><text id='.22624'>/</text><text id='.22625'><code>MemoryMax</code></text><text id='.22634'> limits, independent of </text><text id='.22658'><code>brain run</code></text><text id='.22667'>'s own budget.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.22681' h='b4050ffe'><paragraph id='.22682' h='ec45776a'><text id='.22683'><bold>Bounded concurrency</bold></text><text id='.22702'>: tier 2 jobs are serialized (one VS Code replay at a time), with a timeout that falls back to tier 0 on expiry rather than blocking the ingest queue.</text></paragraph></listItem></list><paragraph id='.12230' rev='10' h='f2ce56f0' heading='2'><text id='.12231'>Known limitations and future work</text></paragraph><list id='.22852' rev='11' h='d4334116' kind='bullet'><listItem id='.22853' h='e467e603'><paragraph id='.22854' h='36391d16'><text id='.22855'>Chrome and the terminal run at tier 0/1 only for this build; the sandboxed-replay design for both is written up above as the path to exact reconstruction, not implemented.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.23026' h='34ebed3d'><paragraph id='.23027' h='7b83d0af'><text id='.23028'>VS Code tier 2 depends on the edited file's content matching at session start; drift from concurrent external edits isn't detected automatically — worth a content-hash check that flags, rather than silently mis-reconstructs, a mismatch.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.23264' h='c4edb4a3'><paragraph id='.23265' h='add586d7'><text id='.23266'><code>text --at T</code></text><text id='.23277'> reconstructs by replaying from session start up to </text><text id='.23329'><code>T</code></text><text id='.23330'>; for a very long-running session this could be slow without periodic snapshots — worth a checkpoint every N events if session lengths in practice warrant it.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.23488' h='9de7d50c'><paragraph id='.23489' h='e76fa52a'><text id='.23490'>The tier 2 adapter interface is intentionally generic — adding Chrome or the terminal back as a full adapter later is a new adapter behind the same interface, not a pipeline redesign.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.23673' h='4c2ae7b6'><paragraph id='.23674' h='266cfd89'><text id='.23675'>Single-user, single-machine design throughout — no multi-user isolation or remote sync, which the assignment doesn't ask for.</text></paragraph></listItem></list><paragraph id='.27289' rev='13' h='94cb36c7' heading='2'><text id='.27290'>Testing and verification plan</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.27319' h='65ea763c'><text id='.27320'>Mapped directly to the assignment's hard requirements:</text></paragraph><list id='.27374' h='2e349512' kind='bullet'><listItem id='.27375' h='8188f65f'><paragraph id='.27376' h='d81ce2c4'><text id='.27377'><bold>No input lag / ≤1% CPU / ≤32MB memory</bold></text><text id='.27414'>: a load-test harness that synthesizes a realistic typing stream (replaying a text corpus at human keystroke timing) while sampling </text><text id='.27546'><code>run</code></text><text id='.27549'>'s CPU and RSS; assert against both thresholds as part of CI, not just checked once by hand.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.27641' h='65435405'><paragraph id='.27642' h='6388426e'><text id='.27643'><bold>Crash safety (</bold></text><text id='.27657'><bold><code>kill -9</code></bold></text><text id='.27664'><bold> + restart causes no corruption)</bold></text><text id='.27696'>: an automated test that starts </text><text id='.27728'><code>run</code></text><text id='.27731'>, feeds it a known stream, sends </text><text id='.27764'><code>SIGKILL</code></text><text id='.27771'> at a randomized point (including mid-write), restarts </text><text id='.27826'><code>run</code></text><text id='.27829'>, and asserts: (a) </text><text id='.27848'><code>PRAGMA integrity_check</code></text><text id='.27870'> passes, (b) the capture log has at most one trailing torn line, (c) re-ingesting from the persisted offset reproduces exactly the events durably appended before the kill — no duplicates, no gaps.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.28066' h='4b42b18d'><paragraph id='.28067' h='3582388f'><text id='.28068'><bold>Live queries see a consistent state</bold></text><text id='.28103'>: a concurrent test running </text><text id='.28131'><code>query</code></text><text id='.28136'> in a tight loop against </text><text id='.28161'><code>run</code></text><text id='.28164'> under continuous synthetic typing, asserting every response is valid JSON representing a state that existed at some real point in time — never a half-written session or a torn reconstruction.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.28356' h='e5202400'><paragraph id='.28357' h='67e705fe'><text id='.28358'><bold>Search semantics</bold></text><text id='.28374'> (a word typed and deleted within a session doesn't count; a word present at session end stays findable even after a later session deletes it elsewhere): unit tests directly against the reconstruction engine covering both halves of that rule.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.28616' h='5ed4547a'><paragraph id='.28617' h='3281a0a1'><text id='.28618'><bold><code>text --at T</code></bold></text><text id='.28629'>: unit tests reconstructing a known keystroke sequence and asserting the output at several points in time matches hand-computed expected state at each </text><text id='.28780'><code>T</code></text><text id='.28781'>.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.28782' h='79ea2e11'><paragraph id='.28783' h='ea751afe'><text id='.28784'><bold>Sleep/wake</bold></text><text id='.28794'>: a test that simulates a </text><text id='.28820'><code>PrepareForSleep</code></text><text id='.28835'> signal pair around continued typing and asserts capture resumes without dropping or duplicating events.</text></paragraph></listItem></list><paragraph id='.28939' h='1357ffd2' heading='2'><text id='.28940'>Decisions log</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.28953' h='608ba42d'><text id='.28954'>Compiled for direct reuse in README.md's "Decisions and edge cases" section.</text></paragraph><list id='.29030' h='4962f75b' kind='bullet'><listItem id='.29031' h='07dc86dc'><paragraph id='.29032' h='492d5193'><text id='.29033'><bold>Target OS</bold></text><text id='.29042'>: Ubuntu/Linux. The assignment's example event uses a macOS-style app id; this build targets Linux throughout (evdev, XKB, GNOME/AT-SPI), with the OS choice called out explicitly since the spec leaves it open.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.29251' h='c92326aa'><paragraph id='.29252' h='552a3c52'><text id='.29253'><bold>Character resolution point</bold></text><text id='.29279'>: resolved at capture time, using live XKB keymap + modifier state, matching the assignment's own example format (</text><text id='.29393'><code>key</code></text><text id='.29396'> + </text><text id='.29399'><code>mods</code></text><text id='.29403'> already resolved, not a raw keycode). Analysis/tier 0 only handles buffer logic (cursor, backspace, selection), not character decoding.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.29539' h='dc882133'><paragraph id='.29540' h='4bb60a65'><text id='.29541'><bold>Capture sink</bold></text><text id='.29553'>: an append-only JSONL log is the durable ground truth; a separate ingest stage (same process) tails it into SQLite+FTS5, which is a derived, rebuildable index. Chosen for the simplest possible crash-safety story, and because the spec treats "collect" and "ingest" as distinct design questions.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.29847' h='9865a02d'><paragraph id='.29848' h='ff91fcec'><text id='.29849'><bold>Session boundaries</bold></text><text id='.29867'>: keyed by </text><text id='.29878'><code>(app, window/context)</code></text><text id='.29899'>; closes after 30s of no keystrokes in that context, or immediately on a commit key (Enter) in single-line contexts. Focus loss alone never ends a session — returning to the same context within the 30s idle window resumes it.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.30124' h='d0c100ad'><paragraph id='.30125' h='1213185c'><text id='.30126'><bold><code>query text</code></bold></text><text id='.30136'><bold> semantics</bold></text><text id='.30146'>: returns the raw decoded keystroke stream (no backspace/cursor editing applied) over a time window — </text><text id='.30248'><code>--at T</code></text><text id='.30254'> (10-minute window ending at T), </text><text id='.30287'><code>--from</code></text><text id='.30293'>/</text><text id='.30294'><code>--to</code></text><text id='.30298'>, or the last 10 minutes by default. Deliberately distinct from </text><text id='.30362'><code>search</code></text><text id='.30368'>'s session-reconstructed, edit-aware text — the two serve different purposes (an audit-style raw trail vs. finding what was actually written).</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.30510' h='1d4ebff5'><paragraph id='.30511' h='da50dcd9'><text id='.30512'><bold>Tier 2 scope</bold></text><text id='.30524'>: VS Code only. Chrome and the terminal get tier 0/1; the full sandboxed-replay design for both is documented as future work rather than built, to keep effort proportionate to the exercise.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.30713' h='8831da7d'><paragraph id='.30714' h='069eaf25'><text id='.30715'><bold><code>text --at &lt;time&gt;</code></bold></text><text id='.30731'><bold> (marked "Level 5" in the PDF)</bold></text><text id='.30761'>: treated as required, not optional — it's explicit in the CLI spec, and levels elsewhere in the PDF weren't legible in extraction.</text></paragraph></listItem></list><paragraph id='.30892' h='5c230cfb' heading='2'><text id='.30893'>Mapping to assignment deliverables</text></paragraph><list id='.30927' h='595e5f5e' kind='bullet'><listItem id='.30928' h='ff30cd95'><paragraph id='.30929' h='31782063'><text id='.30930'><bold>PLAN.md</bold></text><text id='.30937'>: Research &amp; Architecture Design maps onto this doc's Data model, Session definition, Capture/ingestion/concurrency, and Reconstruction pipeline sections — including a comparison of capture approaches (evdev raw input vs. X11 XRecord vs. accessibility-bus hooking vs. a kernel-level driver) for PLAN.md's "how you collect" question, weighing latency, permission model and portability for each.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.31330' h='0590a693'><paragraph id='.31331' h='7f29a0f4'><text id='.31332'><bold>README.md</bold></text><text id='.31341'>: build/run instructions and a supported-OS statement (Ubuntu/Linux, with the exact capture-permission steps — typically adding the user to the </text><text id='.31485'><code>input</code></text><text id='.31490'> group for </text><text id='.31501'><code>/dev/input/event*</code></text><text id='.31518'> access); the Decisions log above, pasted or linked into "Decisions and edge cases."</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.31602' h='fe693473'><paragraph id='.31603' h='4ca3be98'><text id='.31604'><bold><code>ai-logs/</code></bold></text><text id='.31612'>: this conversation, exported in full — it's the actual design-and-decision transcript the assignment asks for; export it alongside any separate agent transcripts from the implementation phase.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.31805' h='2c08bec0'><paragraph id='.31806' h='c8d3379d'><text id='.31807'><bold><code>CLAUDE.md</code></bold></text><text id='.31816'> for the implementing agent: project context (a link to this design doc), the Decisions log, the hard requirements (≤1% CPU, ≤32MB, crash safety, live queries), and a pointer to the Testing and verification plan above so the agent writes verification alongside implementation rather than after it.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.32113' h='d10bf006'><paragraph id='.32114' h='0f4eb313'><text id='.32115'><bold>Code</bold></text><text id='.32119'>: structured per this design's component boundaries (capture, ingest, reconstruction engine with pluggable tiers, query) so each is independently testable, matching the "Architecture &amp; Code Quality" evaluation criterion.</text></paragraph></listItem></list><paragraph id='.32339' rev='14' h='cb8e1321' heading='2'><text id='.32340'>Instructions for the implementing agent</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.32379' h='0d97b382'><text id='.32380'>Build this in four stages, in order. After each stage, the binary must build and run, and that stage's pieces must work end to end — not partially, not stubbed. Test it yourself before handing it back; then stop and wait for explicit sign-off before starting the next stage. Do not start stage N+1 until told the current stage is approved.</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.32719' h='b77f9cbc'><text id='.32720'><bold>Stage 1 — Capture.</bold></text><text id='.32738'> </text><text id='.32739'><code>brain run</code></text><text id='.32748'> captures keystrokes with full context (app, window, resolved key, modifiers, timestamp) and focus-change events, writing them durably per the Data model and Capture, ingestion and concurrency sections above, backed by a working SQL store. No query support yet. Test: run it, type in a few different apps, inspect the capture log and the DB directly, confirm every keystroke and focus change landed with correct context and no gaps or corruption after both a normal stop and a </text><text id='.33225'><code>kill -9</code></text><text id='.33232'> + restart.</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.33243' h='8cea4f05'><text id='.33244'><bold>Stage 2 — Query.</bold></text><text id='.33260'> Implement </text><text id='.33271'><code>brain query search</code></text><text id='.33289'> and </text><text id='.33294'><code>brain query text</code></text><text id='.33310'> per the CLI reference above, against Stage 1's stored data (</text><text id='.33371'><code>search</code></text><text id='.33377'> can run against raw/un-reconstructed text for now if full session reconstruction isn't built yet — say clearly in your test report which parts are real reconstruction vs. a placeholder). Test: run </text><text id='.33575'><code>run</code></text><text id='.33578'> and </text><text id='.33583'><code>query</code></text><text id='.33588'> concurrently, confirm both commands return correct, well-formed JSON matching the spec's shapes while capture keeps going.</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.33711' h='e9b12a44'><text id='.33712'><bold>Stage 3 — Reconstruction.</bold></text><text id='.33737'> Implement the session-definition rules, the tier 0/1/2 reconstruction pipeline and worker pool from Reconstruction pipeline / Safety above, wiring </text><text id='.33885'><code>search</code></text><text id='.33891'>'s session text and </text><text id='.33911'><code>text</code></text><text id='.33915'>'s point-in-time behavior to real reconstructed output. Test: exercise the session-boundary rules directly (idle timeout, commit keys, focus-loss-and-return), the tier 2 VS Code adapter, and the search-semantics edge case (deleted within a session vs. deleted after that session closed).</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.34202' h='daee1103'><text id='.34203'><bold>Stage 4 — End-to-end testing.</bold></text><text id='.34232'> Run the full Testing and verification plan above — performance budget, crash safety, live-query consistency, search semantics, </text><text id='.34360'><code>text --at T</code></text><text id='.34371'>, sleep/wake — against the complete system, not component-by-component in isolation.</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.34455' h='4077eff5'><text id='.34456'><bold>After every stage:</bold></text></paragraph><list id='.34474' h='12d6af51' kind='bullet'><listItem id='.34475' h='e9982cfe'><paragraph id='.34476' h='aed28165'><text id='.34477'>The app must build and actually run — no half-finished stage left in a broken state.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.34561' h='d553d66f'><paragraph id='.34562' h='f6f43eb1'><text id='.34563'>Test it yourself and report what you tested and the result, before reporting the stage done.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.34655' h='9da38778'><paragraph id='.34656' h='aa3478a9'><text id='.34657'>Then stop. Wait for the user to test it and explicitly approve before starting the next stage.</text></paragraph></listItem></list><paragraph id='.34751' h='60a2a55c'><text id='.34752'><bold>Git</bold></text><text id='.34755'>: commit as you go, with descriptive messages in the style of </text><text id='.34817'><link href='https://cbea.ms/git-commit/'>cbea.ms/git-commit</link></text><text id='.34835'> — a short imperative subject line (≤50 characters, no trailing period), a blank line, then a body (wrapped around 72 characters) explaining what changed and why, not how. Commit at meaningful checkpoints within a stage, not only once per stage.</text></paragraph></doc>","complete":true},"frame":{"slug":"de6a18c4-469c-48a4-9673-aa9660495c20","url":"https://claude.ai/artifact/UTxCHmXHeSBaQASSNNiSP9","artifactUrl":"https://claude.ai/artifact/UTxCHmXHeSBaQASSNNiSP9"}}

This doc takes suggestions. Rewording what is already here ("tighten", "improve", "review", "clean up", "this is clunky") → lay the changes in the doc as suggestions: guide( items = ["topic.index", "topic.suggestions"] ) first. Small fixes met on the way (a typo) go in as suggestions with them. The same when asked to suggest, propose or mark up, when the person wants to look before it goes in, and for a question that invites changes ("Any suggestions?", "What would you change?"): suggestions in the doc on this reply, not a list in chat and not comments. An ask for only an exact fix (a typo, a stated fact), new content, a delete or a move → save the edit as usual; the same in a doc you made in this chat, for a rewrite of the whole doc, when told to edit directly, and on an unattended run. While the person is in Suggesting mode, lay it as a suggestion. The person tells you in chat to go ahead with suggestions you laid on an earlier reply ("go ahead", "do the second one") → accept the ones they mean, one call each (topic.suggestions), rather than write the change again. Any other question about the doc → answer in chat.

